 ## Overview
-Valley Cell Simplification is a topological noise reduction method that removes insignificant "valley cells" from a decomposition of a scalar field (typically a 2D density plot for transfer function design) by merging adjacent cells based on boundary persistence [S1]. It solves the problem of over-segmentation caused by noise, where small, semantically irrelevant features fragment the data space [S1]. Unlike standard watershed transformations which produce a flat partition, this method operates hierarchically, allowing the user to control the level of detail via a persistence threshold, effectively filtering out noise while preserving significant peaks [S1].
+Valley Cell Simplification is a topological noise reduction method that removes insignificant "valley cells" from a Morse complex decomposition by merging adjacent cells based on boundary persistence thresholds [S1]. It differs from standard watershed transformations by operating in a hierarchical sense, using persistence to determine the order of merges, thereby preserving significant semantic features while eliminating noise-induced artifacts [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: A set of valley cells $C = \{c_1, c_2, \dots, c_n\}$ forming a partition of the domain, and the boundary persistence value $P(b)$ for every boundary $b$ separating two adjacent cells [S1].
-- Output: A simplified set of valley cells $C'$ where cells with boundary persistence below a threshold $\tau$ have been merged [S1].
+*   **Input:** A set of valley cells derived from a Morse complex decomposition of a scalar field (or 2D density plot), where each cell contains a local maximum. Each boundary between adjacent cells is associated with a boundary persistence value [S1].
+*   **Output:** A simplified set of valley cells where noisy, small cells have been merged into larger, significant clusters [S1].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- **Cell:** Contains a set of local maxima associated with the cell [S1].
-- **Boundary:** Connects two adjacent cells. Stores the persistence value $P$ [S1].
-- **Priority Queue:** Stores all boundaries, ordered by ascending persistence value [G].
+*   **Valley Cells:** Each cell $C_i$ maintains a set of local maxima $M_i$ and a list of adjacent boundaries [S1].
+*   **Boundaries:** Each boundary $B_{ij}$ connects two adjacent cells $C_i$ and $C_j$. It stores a persistence value $P_{ij}$, which measures the separability of the peaks on the adjacent cells [S1].
+*   **Priority Queue:** A structure storing all boundaries, ordered by ascending persistence value [S1].
 
